    "turn_text": "So look, I think there needs to be accountability. And what that accountability looks like, we have to see. We're laws broken with some of this purposeful that their actions foreseeably result in loss of life. There's obviously a lot of protections in place for people who are in public service for carrying out their duty and good faith. We deal with that issue all the time. We're talking about qualified immunity law enforcement and the debates they're in. They're conservatives. who think that maybe that qualified immunity goes too far. I tend to want to preserve and protect the ability it comps to do their job, but it is a balancing act. I think that same thing holds true for all public servants, right? When you have action that you're taking and it is foreseeable that your action will cause harm, then what is the consequence to that? When you let out a criminal, when you are putting criminals on our street, You're either doing it knowingly, or you certainly should have known, in the case of border security, in the context of this, where you're literally you're applying a statute, the Face Act, where 97% of the Face Act prosecutions between 94 and 24 were initiated against pro-life Americans. That is not equal protection laws. Meanwhile, since the Dobbs decision, there's been, let me find it here, 91 pregnancy resource centers that have been attacked What have they been doing? So that bias is very clear and obvious. You know, is that what does that accountability look like? Well, you've got to expose it. And then, you know, there can be, sometimes there can be accountability. There are things that you're seeing when President Trump right now will be voting security clearances. making, you know, certainly firing all these bureaucrats and getting them out of their current roles in public service. I support all of that. Whether there's any kind of prosecutions that should occur, you know, that gets a little trickier. But we need to look and see where 