    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, they certainly want to use the Constitution as a tool to achieve whatever objective they want to achieve politically. But what's new? So they've always done. They did it with Rome, right? They manufactured a quote unquote, right? That exists in the Constitution. The Supreme Court rightfully took the years, rightfully reversed that. So now, I think it is important that we push the envelope and press forward on this whole notion, I'll call it the false notion of birthright citizenship for any individual that is in our country, regardless of their allegiances, regardless of the jurisdiction to which they are subject, which is, importantly, a key phrase in the 14th Amendment, the United States Constitution. That phrase is often forgotten by my colleagues on the other side of the aisle. And in the history of that, for what it's worth, those amendments were passed in 1868, for that amendment I should say. a mere 40 years later, the United States Supreme Court issued an opinion in the slaughterhouse cases. It was that a very specific quote that explicitly stated that it was not to be applied to those that were born here, of people who were subject to foreign nations. In other words, they were here visiting our country, and that the Supreme Court was explicit in the slaughterhouse cases. And then I think it was maybe a decade later, the Supreme Court effectively reinforced that, the name of the case escaping me. And then you had a decade after that in the 1890s, the Kim Wong arc or whatever it is case, where the court I think got it wrong. But even in getting it wrong in applying birthright citizenship to a foreign national here, they only focused it on a legal permanent resident, somebody that was They had permanence, and by the way, I believe that was dicta. I mean, you might look at it, pretty sure it was dicta. So in any event, my point remains, you got one street port depending they're grabbing hold of, and they want to hold on to that. But none of this had anything to do with what they were trying to do to reverse Dred Scott. And that was the whole point. Yet human beings who are in our country, born here, stuck to the position of this country. and subject to the jurisdiction so much so that they were enslaved, right? The laws were being applied to them in that way. And these people were born here and this was coming in to say, you know what? You're a citizen. You're not a citizen because somebody paid some broker to fly you here from China to then deliver a baby and then make it a cottage industry to get American citizenship and you fly back home. That is unconscionable.",
